Ossuary Bounce
Ingredients: NU METAL & RAP METAL + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + 808
Creative Catalyst: It was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule.

Ossuary Bounce welds the downtuned, percussive blunt-force trauma of late-90s rap metal to the eerie spatial dread of spectral horror and booming, blown-out 808s. It sounds like an abandoned gymnasium hosting a ghost-inhabited cypher: subterranean sub-bass rattling structural steel, detuned seven-string guitars ground through decayed magnetic tape, and spitfire rhythmic rhymes that suddenly dissolve into breathless, haunted recitations.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 84–94 BPM. A dragging, neck-snapping half-time swing where rhythmic rap-metal downbeats collide with sluggish, elongated 808 decay tails.
- Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and Locrian modes soaked in cold tritones, dissonant minor-second guitar scrapes, and detuned sub-bass glides that destabilize the root key.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. EVP Gate Flanging: Cavernous spring reverb applied to vocal breaths and guitar chugs, abruptly hard-gated to mimic sudden electronic voice phenomena on ruined tape.
  2. Sub-Displacement Glides: Tuned 808 kicks overdriven through vintage cassette preamps that drop down entire octaves as guitar harmonics pitch-shift upward.
  3. Magnet-Rot Filtering: Heavy high-frequency flutter and wow modulation across master bus stems to simulate decaying magnetic tape unspooling in real time.
- Dynamics: Creeping, minimalist verses driven solely by clicking hi-hats, sub-rumble, and paranoiac whispering, violently rupturing into concussive, double-tracked chug riffs and floor-shaking 808 detonations.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement broke out in 2022 along the rusted canal towns of the upper Ohio River Valley after urban explorers pried open a 1999 municipal time capsule buried beneath a condemned middle school. Inside, among rotted yearbooks, sat a warped Maxell cassette labeled PARISH_DEMO_REJECT; its demagnetized, brine-damaged tracks yielded sludgy, half-erased rap-metal riffs fused with uncanny analog hiss and bottom-heavy tape roar. Local beatmakers and skate-park metalheads obsessively digitized the tape, treating its pitch-wobbles and eerie degradation as a holy aesthetic text. The resulting subculture adopted a uniform of oversized water-repellent windbreakers, frayed vintage denim caked in creek clay, and hand-stitched burial hoods worn over beat-up baseball caps. Gigs occur exclusively in off-grid subterranean spaces—culverts, decommissioned railway bypasses, and defunct cold-storage lockers—where portable PA systems are tuned until the sub-bass rattles dust from the ceiling rafters.

Typical Instruments
- Guitars: 7-string baritone electric guitars tuned to Drop-A or Drop-F, run through high-gain fuzz and pitch-shifter pedals.
- Drums & Rhythm: Modified Roland TR-808 drum machine (pushed through saturated tape heads), layered with physical acoustic snare rims and acoustic ride-cymbal chokes.
- Electronics & Sampling: Roland SP-404 sampler triggering field recordings of decrepit machinery, tape hiss loops, and reverse-EVP voice snippets.
- Vocals: Dual-channel vocal chain pairing an aggressive dynamic microphone (for percussive, syncopated rap-metal bars) with a heavily reverberated condenser (for spectral, breathless hooks).

For Fans Of: Deftones, Ghostemane, early Korn, City Morgue.

